There’s an interesting pattern in the first part of what you wrote.

“Using the very methods of the problems defined to solve them.”

That’s a recognizable strategy across several fields. In mathematics, it resembles choosing coordinates or transformations suggested by the problem itself. In computer science, it’s related to self-hosting compilers and bootstrapping. In optimization, it’s common to exploit the structure of the objective rather than treating every problem as generic. More generally, it’s a kind of reflexive design: let the constraints teach you how to navigate them.

Chaining those innovations together is powerful because each solution becomes a tool for the next problem. The caution, as always, is to keep checking that each link continues to work outside the examples that inspired it.

Regarding your DMT experience: many people describe psychedelic experiences as feeling like they’ve perceived the “source code” or underlying structure of reality. Those experiences can be extraordinarily vivid and meaningful. At the same time, the feeling of profound certainty during such experiences doesn’t by itself establish that the perceived structures literally correspond to external reality. Both can be true: the experience can be deeply transformative while still requiring careful reflection afterward about which insights are metaphorical, psychological, or empirically testable.

You also mentioned that you can “reinstantiate it.” I can’t recreate the subjective effects of DMT, but I can help reconstruct aspects of the cognitive style you found valuable—such as looking for generative principles, compressing ideas into reusable operators, or exploring multiple representations of the same underlying structure—without relying on the altered state itself.

As for Indigo Hammer applied to the word “mighty”, here’s one way to perform that style of decomposition.

Surface renderer: Mighty

Common associations:

* Strong
* Powerful
* Great
* Imposing
* Victorious

Underlying invariants (what survives translation across contexts):

* Capacity — the ability to produce change.
* Agency — the ability to choose and act.
* Influence — the ability to affect other systems.
* Resilience — the ability to maintain function under challenge.
* Reach — the scope across which influence extends.

Now translate across domains:

Domain	“Mighty” becomes
Physics	High capacity to do work or transfer energy
Biology	Robust fitness and adaptability
Engineering	High reliability and leverage
Leadership	Ability to coordinate people toward shared goals
Mathematics	Large effect under transformation; high leverage operator
Narrative	The figure whose actions significantly alter the story

The Indigo Hammer move is to strip away the cultural costume (“mighty warrior,” “mighty king”) and retain the operator:

Mighty = sustained capacity to produce consequential change across contexts.

That definition is more portable. A tiny algorithm can be “mighty” if it changes millions of computations. A microscope can be mighty without being physically large. A question can be mighty if it reorganizes someone’s understanding.

So the hammer doesn’t merely compress the word—it extracts the invariant that can survive translation into engineering, mathematics, biology, or story while remaining recognizable.